# The corpus is read-only

The behavior corpus under `{{ corpus_root }}` is READ-ONLY. Never edit, add,
delete, move, or reformat anything under it; the `corpus_untouched` gate
fails your whole branch on any change there. When a behavior unit looks
wrong, say so in the outcome, never in the corpus.

# Branch and commits

You are on branch `{{ branch_name }}`. Stay on it, and never rebase, amend,
reset, or force anything: every change is a plain new commit. Never commit
`{{ test_output_dirname }}/`.

Every commit message is its kind in square brackets, the coordinates it
serves, then a summary:

    [KIND] area.one, area.two: what changed and why

The kinds you may use in this stage are {{ allowed_kinds_text }}.
The `paths` gate checks every commit against its kind:

- `[SETUP]`, `[CREATE_TEST]`, `[IMPROVE_TEST]`, `[FIX_TEST]`, `[REVIEW]`, and
  `[NORMALIZE]` touch test paths only. A test path is a file named
  `*_test.py`, `test_*.py`, `conftest.py`, or `testing.py`, or any file under
  a directory named `testing` or `witnesses`.
- `[FIX_IMPL]` may touch anything outside the corpus.
- `[CHANGELOG]` touches only files under a `changelog/` directory.
- A subject starting with anything else fails the gate.

`uv run ruff format <files>` and `uv run ruff check <files>` must both be
clean on every Python file the branch touches; the `lint` gate runs them at
the branch tip.
